      "reasoning": "Wait! I have TWO Disciple of Perdition entries in my hand! One is p10 (on battlefield) and one is in my hand! That's odd, but let me focus on combat!\n\
\n\
*Eyes glow with malicious intent*\n\
\n\
COMBAT PHASE! I have:\n\
- Bone Picker (3/2 flying, SICK - can't attack!)\n\
- Disciple of Perdition (1/3, can attack)\n\
- Durable Coilbug (2/2, can attack)\n\
- Wakedancer (2/2, can attack)\n\
\n\
Wait - Bone Picker is SICK? That means summoning sickness! I just cast it this turn, so it can't attack!\n\
\n\
But I should attack with Disciple, Coilbug, and Wakedancer! All three!\n\
\n\
But wait - let me think strategically. What does the opponent have:\n\
- Preston (2/5, can't attack next turn since it's tapped, will untap)\n\
- Brightmare (2/3, sick, won't be able to attack)\n\
- Unicorn (2/2, sick, won't be able to attack)\n\
\n\
So they have 2/5 + 2/3 + 2/2 = 6/10 power total, but none of them can attack next turn except... they might untap Preston!\n\
\n\
Actually, I have 1+2+2 = 5 power attacking. The opponent might block with Brightmare (2/3), Unicorn (2/2), or Preston (2/5). \n\
\n\
But more importantly - the Bone Picker gives me FLYING! Even though it's sick this turn, next turn it can attack and fly over all their creatures!\n\
\n\
Let me declare all three creatures as attackers!",
